{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: Matplotlib \n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Why Matplotlib?\n",
        "\n",
        "## What's Matplotlib?\n",
        "\n",
        "> [Matplotlib is a comprehensive library for creating static, animated, and interactive visualizations.](https://matplotlib.org/stable/)\n",
        "\n",
        "* **MAT**LAB **Plot** **Lib**rary\n",
        "* Based on the \"industry standard\" library that predates Python\n",
        "* Basis of many more modern tools (namely [Seaborn](https://seaborn.pydata.org/tutorial/objects_interface.html))\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why Matplotlib\n",
        "\n",
        "* Versus its closest competitors (Altair, Seaborn, ggplot2, Plotly):\n",
        "  * Allows dramatically more control over created plots.\n",
        "  * Extremely good NumPy and pandas (our next library) integration.\n",
        "  * Wealth of resources\n",
        "  * Entirely free and open-source\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why not Matplotlib\n",
        "\n",
        "- Altair and Plotly have far better web integration and interaction.\n",
        "- Seaborn has beautiful defaults.\n",
        "- Statisticians like ggplot2, which is from another language (R)\n",
        "- Many modern data visualizations go on websites, which is not to Matplotlib's strengths.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Relevance\n",
        "\n",
        "- We have been working with a piecewise function for sometime!\n",
        "- Can we finally visualize it?\n",
        "\n",
        "# Install\n",
        "\n",
        "## pip like NumPy\n",
        "\n",
        "- Just like NumPy, Matplotlib is a Python package which we install via `pip`\n",
        "```{.bash code-line-numbers=\"false\"}\n",
        "python3 -m pip install matplotlib \n",
        "```\n",
        "- That might take a moment, when it does we can check it worked!\n",
        "\n",
        "## Verify\n",
        "\n",
        "- We can quickly verify installation and introduce some conventions.\n",
        "- Open up Python and import the libraries:\n"
      ],
      "id": "f1ae3c23"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
      ],
      "id": "4c319211",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Plotting\n",
        "\n",
        "- Let's plot something\n",
        "\n",
        "> [A period is a horizontal row of the periodic table. There are seven periods in the periodic table, with each one beginning at the far left. A new period begins when a new principal energy level begins filling with electrons.](https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Introductory_Chemistry/Introductory_Chemistry_(CK-12)/06%3A_The_Periodic_Table/6.04%3A_Modern_Periodic_Table-_Periods_and_Groups)\n",
        "\n",
        "## The Table\n",
        "\n",
        "<a style=\"filter:invert(.9)\" title=\"Cepheus, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons\" href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Periodic_table.svg\"><img width=\"100%\" alt=\"Periodic Table\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Periodic_table.svg\"></a>\n",
        "\n",
        "## Rows\n",
        "\n",
        "> [Period 1 has only two elements (hydrogen and helium), while periods 2 and 3 have 8 elements. Periods 4 and 5 have 18 elements. Periods 6 and 7 have 32 elements, because the two bottom rows that are separate from the rest of the table belong to those periods.](https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Introductory_Chemistry/Introductory_Chemistry_(CK-12)/06%3A_The_Periodic_Table/6.04%3A_Modern_Periodic_Table-_Periods_and_Groups)\n",
        "\n",
        " This also happens to be the maximum number of electrons in the outermost \"shell\" of an atom.\n"
      ],
      "id": "d93bf8ae"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "es = np.array([2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32, 32])"
      ],
      "id": "df300493",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Plots\n",
        "\n",
        "- Creating a naive (that is, specifying no options) plot is *very* easy.\n"
      ],
      "id": "d94df5fc"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.plot(es)"
      ],
      "id": "dfafd4a3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- Wait a minute - we're at the command line... where does the image go?\n",
        "\n",
        "## Command Line\n",
        "\n",
        "- When I do this at the command line, I usually see the following:\n",
        "```{.bash code-line-numbers=\"false\"}\n",
        ">>> plt.plot(a)\n",
        "[<matplotlib.lines.Line2D object at 0x7f34f7094cd0>]\n",
        "```\n",
        "- That is... not a chart.\n",
        "- No worries!\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Making Charts\n",
        "\n",
        "- My preferred way to work with charts is by *saving them as an image file*.\n",
        "  \t- Can include them as attachments in emails.\n",
        "\t- Can incorporate them into scientific writing.\n",
        "\t- Can post interesting findings on social media.\n",
        "- We simply save the file.\n",
        "\t- I always save a \".svg\" file - \"scalable vector graphic\"\n",
        "\t- These don't get fuzzy when you zoom in.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Saving Charts\n",
        "\n",
        "- I use the following to save my chart as a \".svg\" image file.\n",
        "```{.python}\n",
        "plt.savefig(\"my_chart.svg\")\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Viewing Charts\n",
        "\n",
        "- I usually exit Python to view charts, or wrote scripts that generate charts and run them at the command line.\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.python filename=\"chart_maker.py\"}\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "es = np.array([2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32, 32])\n",
        "plt.plot(es)\n",
        "plt.savefig(\"my_chart.svg\")\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "## Open Image\n",
        "\n",
        "- On Windows, view image from terminal:\n",
        "```{.bash code-line-numbers=\"false\"}\n",
        "nvim chart_maker.py # code from last slide\n",
        "python chart_maker.py\n",
        "start my_chart.svg\n",
        "```\n",
        "- On MacOS, view image from terminal:\n",
        "```{.bash code-line-numbers=\"false\"}\n",
        "nvim chart_maker.py # code from last slide\n",
        "python3 chart_maker.py \n",
        "open my_chart.svg\n",
        "```\n",
        "- Should open in your default image viewer.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Reference\n",
        "\n",
        "- Recall: We should see this:"
      ],
      "id": "3bd812db"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.plot(es) # how this was made"
      ],
      "id": "6e30e5be",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# OS\n",
        "\n",
        "## The OS package\n",
        "\n",
        "- I often use one other `import` when working with images.\n",
        "- Often times, I end up with a NumPy array where I'm trying various ways of plotting, and don't want to close Python.\n",
        "- I could open another terminal tab, but there's another option:"
      ],
      "id": "bde04715"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import os # stands for \"operating system\""
      ],
      "id": "17d563e7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Using OS\n",
        "\n",
        "- OS let's us do a lot of the things we do in the shell without leaving Python.\n",
        "- The most useful technique is `os.system()` which allows us to run a shell command from within Python.\n",
        "- For example, the following would open \"my_chart.svg\" on MacOS \n",
        "```{.python}\n",
        "os.system(\"open my_chart.svg\")\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "## On OS\n",
        "\n",
        "- Historically, an operating system was considered synonmous with its command line.\n",
        "- This is reflected within how we use the `os` module with the requirement to use `start` on Windows and `open` on MacOS.\n",
        "- In both cases, the same file is opened in a photo viewer, but\n",
        "- The command differs due to the different *operating system* (OS)\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Use of OS \n",
        "\n",
        "- The `os` module has largely fallen out of favor versus `subprocess`, which more robust but harder to use.\n",
        "- As with Matplotlib vs. e.g. Altair or Seaborn, I teach the older, easier, less-snazzy version.\n",
        "- In general, `os` is no longer recommended for use outside of scientific computing.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Another example\n",
        "\n",
        "- By the way, you now know how to:\n",
        "  - Open `nvim`, write a file, and save it\n",
        "  - `import` that file\n",
        "  - Run the contents of that file to create an image and\n",
        "  - View the image\n",
        "- All without leaving Python!\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plot Elements \n",
        "\n",
        "## Piecewise \n",
        "\n",
        "- We return now to the income tax example to show some ways of plotting.\n",
        "- Let's recall the `taxes` array quickly."
      ],
      "id": "de63d6a7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "taxes = np.array([\n",
        "    [9275, .1],\n",
        "    [37650, .15],\n",
        "    [91150, .25],\n",
        "    [190150, .28],\n",
        "    [413350, .33],\n",
        "    [415051, .35]\n",
        "])"
      ],
      "id": "db6ad3be",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Naive Plotting\n",
        "\n",
        "- This is probably not what we intended! "
      ],
      "id": "3b6063e0"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.plot(taxes)"
      ],
      "id": "34b84806",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## x and y\n",
        "\n",
        "- Plot \"cutoff vs rate\" by providing both."
      ],
      "id": "92994c22"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "x = taxes[:,0]\n",
        "y = taxes[:,1]\n",
        "plt.plot(x,y)"
      ],
      "id": "8fb8e365",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Plotting Functions\n",
        "\n",
        "- Really though, we want to:\n",
        "  - Create an array of possible incomes.\n",
        "  - Calculate tax at that income.\n",
        "  - Plot that tax.\n",
        "- This is plotting a function!\n",
        "\n",
        "## The function\n",
        "\n",
        "- Spoilers for earlier exercises.\n"
      ],
      "id": "3cd83d05"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| code-fold: true\n",
        "taxes = [\n",
        "    [9275, 0.1, 0.0],\n",
        "    [37650, 0.15, -463.75],\n",
        "    [91150, 0.25, -4228.75],\n",
        "    [190150, 0.28, -6963.25],\n",
        "    [413350, 0.33, -16470.75],\n",
        "    [415051, 0.35, -24737.75],\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "def single_tax(income):\n",
        "    for tax in taxes:\n",
        "        if income < tax[0]:\n",
        "            return income * tax[1] + tax[2]\n",
        "    return income * .396 + -43830.05"
      ],
      "id": "52045838",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Creating Arrays\n",
        "\n",
        "- Let's consider some possible incomes.\n",
        "- We can use `np.arange()` to create a range of values using the same idea as *slices*\n",
        "  \t- Start\n",
        "\t- Stop\n",
        "\t- Step"
      ],
      "id": "a31c7c5a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "incomes = np.arange(10000,400000,100000)\n",
        "incomes"
      ],
      "id": "77832414",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## linspace\n",
        "\n",
        "- `np.linspace()` is a bit more common and may be easier.\n",
        "- Give a start, stop, and a number of values..."
      ],
      "id": "e0bfab79"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "incomes = np.linspace(10000,400000,5)\n",
        "incomes"
      ],
      "id": "20317af7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Aside: 0s and 1s\n",
        "\n",
        "- More generally, we can create arrays with `np.ones()` or `np.zeros()`\n",
        "- Just provide a length.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::: {.columns}\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"50%\"}\n"
      ],
      "id": "9aa2751e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "np.ones(3)"
      ],
      "id": "124f1f05",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"50%\"}\n"
      ],
      "id": "ce7a2230"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "np.zeros(2)"
      ],
      "id": "6922abdd",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Aside: `dtype`\n",
        "\n",
        "- By default, these are floating point values."
      ],
      "id": "3c079ade"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "np.ones(3) / np.arange(2,5,1)"
      ],
      "id": "53a9d8b8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- You can get integers by specifying a NumPy `dtype` (for data type)"
      ],
      "id": "d672bc03"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "np.ones(3, dtype=int) // np.arange(2,5,1)"
      ],
      "id": "3c6422ea",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- *Always* think about whether you want round numbers or not.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Plotting Taxation\n",
        "\n",
        "- Let's make a linspace from, say, `1` to `500000`."
      ],
      "id": "5541d205"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "incomes = np.linspace(1,500000,100)"
      ],
      "id": "dfe18dfd",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Vectorizing\n",
        "\n",
        "- Unlike, say `+` and `-`, `single_tax` is not a built-in, vectorized operation in NumPy.\n",
        "- Not to worry, we just ask NumPy to `np.vectorize` it!"
      ],
      "id": "6af6792a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "vector_tax = np.vectorize(single_tax)\n",
        "costs = vector_tax(incomes)\n",
        "costs[::20] # Just look at 5 examples"
      ],
      "id": "1e55c94f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Plot Vector Functions\n",
        "\n",
        "- Let's take a look!"
      ],
      "id": "0fc3dc14"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.plot(incomes,costs)"
      ],
      "id": "196b85e6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## More fun\n",
        "\n",
        "- We can also plot tax *rate*"
      ],
      "id": "dce36fc9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.plot(incomes, costs / incomes)"
      ],
      "id": "4782489f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Or Both\n"
      ],
      "id": "b9d01740"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.plot(incomes, costs)\n",
        "plt.plot(incomes, costs / incomes)"
      ],
      "id": "1e74472e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- Probably should put them on different scales!\n",
        "\n",
        "# Chart Elements\n",
        "\n",
        "## Well-formed Charts\n",
        "\n",
        "- I learned charts should have:\n",
        "\t- Labels on the vertical and horizontal axes\n",
        "\t- A title\n",
        "\t- A legend\n",
        "- Let's add these.\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "- `plt.title`\n"
      ],
      "id": "45b7b6f4"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.title(\"Income Tax % by Income (USD)\")\n",
        "plt.plot(incomes, costs / incomes)"
      ],
      "id": "e4b211bd",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "##  \n",
        "\n",
        "- `plt.xlabel` and `plt.ylabel`\n"
      ],
      "id": "5881e244"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.title(\"Income Tax % by Income (USD)\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Income in USD\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Percent Tax Rate\")\n",
        "plt.plot(incomes, costs / incomes)"
      ],
      "id": "aa5f0b33",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "##   \n",
        "\n",
        "- `plt.legend` \n"
      ],
      "id": "f835de83"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.title(\"Income Tax % by Income (USD)\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Income in USD\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Percent Tax Rate\")\n",
        "plt.plot(incomes, costs / incomes)\n",
        "plt.legend() # We didn't label any of our plots!"
      ],
      "id": "57dbec51",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "##   \n",
        "\n",
        "- `label=`\n"
      ],
      "id": "69d83acf"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.title(\"Income Tax % by Income (USD)\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Income in USD\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Percent Tax Rate\")\n",
        "plt.plot(incomes, costs / incomes * 100, label = \"Tax/Income\")\n",
        "plt.legend() # This shows the labels "
      ],
      "id": "af2c88ae",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Annotation\n",
        "\n",
        "- We'll add a note where median income (about 40k) is."
      ],
      "id": "728cc1ec"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "med_inc = 40000\n",
        "med_tax = single_tax(40000)\n",
        "med_pct = med_tax / med_inc * 100\n",
        "xy = [med_inc, med_pct]\n",
        "xy"
      ],
      "id": "b9e17558",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- We'll use `plt.annotate`\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "- `plt.annotate()`\n"
      ],
      "id": "851f7798"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.plot(incomes, costs / incomes * 100, label = \"Tax/Income\")\n",
        "plt.annotate(\"Median Income\", xy)"
      ],
      "id": "cbfaa2dc",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Using functions\n",
        "\n",
        "- We can use functions to add anotations."
      ],
      "id": "79b0d654"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def add_note(inc, note):\n",
        "\ttax = single_tax(inc)\n",
        "\tpct = tax / inc * 100\n",
        "\txy = [inc, pct]\n",
        "\tplt.annotate(note, xy)"
      ],
      "id": "9319d99e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "##\n"
      ],
